Title: What Quicklisp requires from an implementation (networking / sockets)

Summary
-------
Question: to make Quicklisp (http://quicklisp.org/) work on a Lisp
implementation, what must the implementation provide? In particular, do we
need to supply networking through a socket portability library?

Answer: No socket portability library (usocket etc.) is needed. Quicklisp
carries its OWN thin per-implementation socket shim plus its own pure-CL
HTTP client, gunzip, and untar, so it has essentially zero external
dependencies. Everything is bootstrapped from (unsigned-byte 8) streams.

Portability mechanism
---------------------
Each portability point is a `ql-impl:definterface` (impl.lisp:77): a
defgeneric dispatching on a singleton instance of an implementation class,
with an optional `(:implementation t)` portable default. To port to a new
implementation you:

  1. Add a `define-implementation-package` clause in impl.lisp: a feature
     keyword, a `:class`, and `:reexport-from` / `:prep` forms exposing that
     Lisp's socket primitives.
  2. Supply methods for the few interfaces that have NO portable `t`
     default.

The only two interfaces with no portable default — MUST be provided
--------------------------------------------------------------------
  1. open-connection (host port)            -- network.lisp:13
     The critical one. Must return an open, bidirectional, BINARY
     (unsigned-byte 8) stream to a TCP host/port. The default `t` method
     just signals "quicklisp in implementation ... is not supported yet."
     This is the single piece of real OS networking Quicklisp needs.

  2. directory-entries (directory)          -- impl-util.lisp:200
     Directory listing. Wild-pathname conventions and symlink handling vary
     too much for a portable default.

Everything else already has a working (:implementation t) default
-----------------------------------------------------------------
Networking (network.lisp), override only if the default is wrong:
  - host-address   (returns the host as-is; override only if your socket
                    layer wants a resolved address rather than a name)
  - read-octets    (read-sequence)
  - write-octets   (write-sequence + finish-output)
  - close-connection (close)
  - call-with-connection

Filesystem (impl-util.lisp), portable defaults provided:
  - rename-directory, probe-directory, native-namestring,
    directory-write-date, directoryp, delete-directory,
    delete-directory-tree, call-with-quiet-compilation
  - init-file-name is per-impl but only powers the optional
    ql:add-to-init-file convenience.

Pure-CL — no implementation or external-tool support needed
-----------------------------------------------------------
  - HTTP client (http.lisp): a self-contained HTTP/1.0 GET-with-redirects
    client built directly on octet streams. `*fetch-scheme-functions*` only
    registers "http" -> http-fetch (http.lisp:795); proxy supported. No
    https in the client itself.
  - Gunzip (deflate.lisp, RFC 1951, Pierre Mai's portable deflate), untar
    (minitar.lisp), and the CDB index reader (cdb.lisp) are all 100%
    portable CL.
  - Confirmed: NO run-program / curl / wget / zlib / libtar shell-out
    anywhere in the client.

Baseline the implementation must meet
-------------------------------------
  - ANSI CL with CLOS (the whole shim is generic functions / methods).
  - Binary streams with :element-type '(unsigned-byte 8), plus
    read-sequence / write-sequence.
  - Working directory / probe-file / pathnames.
  - ASDF (Quicklisp drives ASDF).
  - Some way to load native sockets at bootstrap (the :prep `(require ...)`
    forms, e.g. sb-bsd-sockets on SBCL).

Bottom line
-----------
To bring up Quicklisp on a new implementation you write roughly ONE function
of real substance — open-connection returning a binary TCP stream — plus
directory-entries, plus a package clause exposing your Lisp's socket
symbols. No socket portability library, no external gzip/tar, no curl.


========================================================================
RESOLUTION (2026-06-30)
========================================================================

Scope was extended beyond the original analysis: rather than only the one
`open-connection` function Quicklisp strictly needs, bocl now ships a full,
exhaustive BSD-socket layer with a usocket-compatible Lisp API, which both
satisfies the Quicklisp requirement and provides a general networking facility.

What was implemented
--------------------
1. C kernel primitives (sources/socket.c, registered via bocl_register_socket()
   in sources/kernel.c, declared in sources/bocl.h, built via sources/Makefile).
   Thin, non-signalling wrappers over the POSIX/BSD sockets API:

     %SOCKET %BIND %CONNECT %LISTEN %ACCEPT %CLOSE-FD %SHUTDOWN
     %GETSOCKNAME %GETPEERNAME %SETSOCKOPT-INT %GETSOCKOPT-INT
     %SETSOCKOPT %GETSOCKOPT %SEND %RECV %SENDTO %RECVFROM %SOCKETPAIR
     %GETADDRINFO %GETNAMEINFO %SOCKADDR-FAMILY %MAKE-SOCKADDR-UN
     %INET-PTON %INET-NTOP %SET-NONBLOCKING %SELECT %MAKE-SOCKET-STREAM
     %SOCKET-ERRNO %GAI-ERRNO %STRERROR %GAI-STRERROR %ERRNO-KEYWORD
     %SOCKET-CONSTANTS

   - All socket domains/types/protocols are reachable: the (DOMAIN TYPE
     PROTOCOL) triple and raw sockaddr octet strings are passed through
     verbatim, so AF_INET / AF_INET6 / AF_UNIX / AF_PACKET / ... and
     SOCK_STREAM / SOCK_DGRAM / SOCK_RAW / SOCK_SEQPACKET all work.
   - Errors are never signalled from C: a sentinel is returned and errno is
     stashed for (%SOCKET-ERRNO); the Lisp layer maps errno -> condition via
     (%ERRNO-KEYWORD).  Constant values (AF_*, SOCK_*, IPPROTO_*, SO_*, MSG_*,
     SHUT_*, AI_*, NI_*, EAI_*) come straight from the platform headers via
     (%SOCKET-CONSTANTS) so the port hard-codes no numbers.
   - A connected socket is given a CL stream by fdopen()-ing the fd unbuffered
     into an ordinary FILE-STREAM (%MAKE-SOCKET-STREAM), so READ-BYTE /
     WRITE-BYTE / READ-CHAR / READ-SEQUENCE / WRITE-SEQUENCE all work through
     the existing stream machinery.

2. usocket-compatible Lisp package (bootstrap/socket.lisp), package
   "BOCL-SOCKET" nicknamed "USOCKET", auto-loaded by the prelude when the
   kernel exposes %SOCKET (guard added at the end of bootstrap/prelude.lisp).
   It implements the usocket public API:
     - classes USOCKET / STREAM-USOCKET / STREAM-SERVER-USOCKET /
       DATAGRAM-USOCKET and their accessors;
     - SOCKET-CONNECT (TCP & UDP, with timeout/nodelay/local-host/local-port),
       SOCKET-LISTEN, SOCKET-ACCEPT, SOCKET-CLOSE, SOCKET-SHUTDOWN;
     - SOCKET-SEND / SOCKET-RECEIVE (datagram);
     - GET-LOCAL/PEER-ADDRESS/PORT/NAME;
     - WAIT-FOR-INPUT + MAKE-WAIT-LIST / ADD-WAITER / REMOVE-WAITER /
       REMOVE-ALL-WAITERS (select-based);
     - the full condition hierarchy (SOCKET-CONDITION / SOCKET-ERROR and the
       ~20 specific subclasses, plus the NS-* name-resolution conditions) with
       errno/EAI_* mapping and WITH-MAPPED-CONDITIONS;
     - the WITH-CONNECTED-SOCKET / WITH-CLIENT-SOCKET / WITH-SERVER-SOCKET /
       WITH-SOCKET-LISTENER macros;
     - the address utilities (DOTTED-QUAD-TO-VECTOR-QUAD,
       VECTOR-QUAD-TO-DOTTED-QUAD, HOST-BYTE-ORDER, HBO-TO-DOTTED-QUAD,
       HBO-TO-VECTOR-QUAD, VECTOR-TO-IPV6-HOST, IPV6-HOST-TO-VECTOR, IP=, ...).

Relation to Quicklisp
---------------------
The one interface Quicklisp has no portable default for, open-connection — "an
open, bidirectional, BINARY (unsigned-byte 8) TCP stream" — is now a one-liner:
  (socket-stream (socket-connect host port :element-type '(unsigned-byte 8)))
read-sequence / write-sequence on that stream are verified working.  The
BOCL-SOCKET internals (%MAKE-SOCKET-STREAM and the connect/listen operators)
are exactly the basis a ql-impl socket clause would re-export.

Verification
------------
End-to-end loopback tests pass: TCP client/server (char + byte + sequence I/O,
local/peer name lookup), UDP datagram both directions (send + sendto/recvfrom),
AF_UNIX stream sockets and socketpair, WAIT-FOR-INPUT readiness, and condition
mapping (connection-refused-error against a dead port).  Regression baselines
(must-condition 142/147, must-data-and-control 309/310, must-array 330,
must-sequence 3851, ...) are unchanged.

Closed: implemented.  Version bumped to 1.4.20.
